    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d897">

                <pb facs="0961" n="1795" xml:id="pg1795"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 656.119/320</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Peru</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >McMillin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lima</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-04-05T10:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 5, 1918, 10 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 4.02 p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Department’s circular of March 23, 7 p.m. In reply to Legation’s note, Minister
                    of Foreign Affairs says that Peruvian Government gives to Government of the
                    United States most complete assurances that the ships which the American
                    Government has decided to dedicate to commercial traffic between the United
                    States and Peru will enjoy [in] Peruvian ports fullest facilities, so that in no
                    case can they be subjected to delays or exigencies of bond arising from
                    litigation which might come up over their possession.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">McMillin</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
